To determine how many lassis Caroline can make out of twelve mangoes, we start by finding out how many lassis she can make with one mango. According to the problem, Caroline can make eleven lassis out of two mangoes. Therefore, the number of lassis she can make with one mango is:

\[
\frac{11 \text{ lassis}}{2 \text{ mangoes}} = 5.5 \text{ lassis per mango}
\]

Next, we need to find out how many lassis she can make with twelve mangoes. We do this by multiplying the number of lassis per mango by the total number of mangoes:

\[
5.5 \text{ lassis per mango} \times 12 \text{ mangoes} = 66 \text{ lassis}
\]

Thus, Caroline can make \boxed{66}

